WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.870
-Our first guest tonight is
an Oscar-nominated, Emmy-winning

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.972
actor you know from movies like
"The Imitation Game,"

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.740
"The Hobbit," and
the "Avengers" franchise,

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.542
as well as
the series "Sherlock."

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:11.477
He stars in
"The Power of the Dog,"

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:12.879
which is in
select theaters today

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:13.947
and on Netflix December 1st.

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:15.448
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:19.318
-Now, gentlemen, look, see --
that's what you with the cloth.

00:00:19.318 --> 00:00:21.954
-Oh!
-Oh!

00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:23.289
-Right.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:26.826
-It's really just for
wine drips.

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.494
-Oh, you got that, boys?

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:31.664
Only for the drips.

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:33.299
[ Laughter ]

00:00:36.135 --> 00:00:38.904
Now get us some food.

00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:40.940
-Please welcome to the show
Benedict Cumberbatch.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.109
Hello, Benedict.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.078
-Hi, Seth. How are you?

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:50.683
-Now, Benedict, I know you
to be a lovely person,

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:54.054
and that is very much not who
you are playing in this film.

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:55.521
-You should see me on
a Monday morning.

00:00:55.521 --> 00:00:59.559
No, I mean, it was a bit of a
reach, to say the least.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:01.427
And, I mean, you know,
it's a strange one.

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:03.996
You get a call from Jane Campion
and you just say, you know,

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:05.164
"What? When? Where?

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:06.465
I'm in."

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:11.170
And then you find out
what it is and go, "Why me?"

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.272
She had faith.

00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:16.376
She knew there was
something about this character,

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:18.277
something that made him
truly vulnerable

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:21.347
underneath the hard exterior of
sort of toxic masculinity

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:23.182
that she thought
I could bring to it.

00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:25.418
And she had every faith that I'd
get the kind of --

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:26.719
the heart and the kismet down.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:30.055
And I have a few experiences
I -- She was amazing.

00:01:30.055 --> 00:01:31.558
She was amazing
in rehearsals, as well,

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:32.925
production, and ever since.

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:33.960
And she said,
"Look, if there's anything

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.061
you're feeling insecure about,

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:36.128
we're here to help you
as production."

00:01:36.128 --> 00:01:37.196
Please let us know."

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:38.731
And I went, "Uh...okay.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:40.966
Banjo playing,
being an ass[bleep]

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:42.602
whittling, whistling.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.637
I mean, just this list of
things this guy has

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.773
lived, breathed, and mastered
over 40-odd years

00:01:47.773 --> 00:01:50.309
of his life on this ranch
in 1925 Montana.

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:51.744
So they took me to dude camp,

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:54.480
and I learned to do everything I
did in the film, pretty much.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.616
-I mean, not only do you get to
work with Jane Campion,

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.784
you get to shoot in New Zealand.

00:01:58.784 --> 00:02:02.055
That must have added to the
many pleasures of this project.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:03.989
-Oh, I mean, you know,
it's her kingdom,

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:05.825
but, my God,
it's such a welcoming place.

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:08.127
Everyone there is sort of
hard-wired to be hospitable.

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:10.562
And that landscape is another
character in the film

00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:13.867
and just a gift for an actor,
especially one playing

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:15.869
a character who's so much
about the outside

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:17.036
and bringing the outside in.

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:18.071
He's a man who
never takes his clothes off.

00:02:18.071 --> 00:02:19.239
He never washes.

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:21.674
He's incredibly buttoned up
and contained

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.077
and dirtified by his work in
a very honest way.

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:27.112
And, you know, the landscape
is also his refuge.

00:02:27.112 --> 00:02:30.783
It's the one place where he can
be private and be true to the

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.685
sort of the secret
that he's hiding

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:35.487
and find this sort of moment
to express that

00:02:35.487 --> 00:02:36.722
and connect with that.

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:38.525
And, at the same time,
it's what he masters --

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:40.993
the weather, the animals, the
men that help him run the ranch.

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:44.630
And to be in a place like that,
which is truly still

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.232
some of the most, I think,
spectacular wilderness

00:02:47.232 --> 00:02:50.236
on the planet, was just
such a gift for this movie.

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:54.140
-So, I heard that you went
a whole week without showering.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.843
Was this a choice that you made
for your character

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:59.078
or did Jane ask this of you?

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:02.781
-It's such a thing,
this sort of people not washing.

00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:04.484
But, yeah, this was for
character work,

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:06.085
I would say, first and foremost.

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:09.355
And it was a request, and my
family hasn't yet arrived

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:10.957
to the country,
so that was helpful.

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:12.291
However, you know,
there was a cleaner

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:13.993
who came in
once or twice a week.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:16.362
And then Jane went,
"Oh, this is great.

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:17.863
So, we're going to meet some
friends for sushi.

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:20.699
And I'm like, "Oh, no.
I'm like a biohazard sign."

00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:23.235
I'm walking 5 feet
in front of me, you know.

00:03:23.235 --> 00:03:25.471
People wanting selfies are
gonna, "Can I have a -- Ugh!"

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:28.073
And just run in
the opposite direction,

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:29.442
which is not
a bad thing sometimes.

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:32.145
But, you know,
in rehearsal and just to feel --

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:33.779
This guy didn't wash
for like months.

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:35.481
So it's pretty tame.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.784
It's big in our
modern-day environment

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:40.018
of cleanliness
and one or two showers --

00:03:40.018 --> 00:03:42.522
open the debate -- a day.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:45.190
But, you know, yeah,
it's quite something to go there

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.192
even just for a week.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:48.060
It's very peculiar.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.229
-So, I heard Jesse Plemons,
who plays your brother,

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:52.498
the incredible actor --
that one of the things

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:56.001
Jane asked was
for you guys to, as actors,

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:57.470
learn each others' smell.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.072
She asked you to waltz together,

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:02.274
to take an
afternoon nap together.

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:04.143
Now, I have to ask,

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:08.114
does it take a director of
Jane Campion's level

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:10.916
for you to say, "Okay."

00:04:10.916 --> 00:04:15.755
-[ Chuckles ]
Uh...yeah, kind of.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:18.190
It's not a request I get much
on the Marvel set.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.659
But, look,
I'd happily take a nap with

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:21.894
Jesse Plemons on any job.

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:23.228
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:25.731
-He's a sweetheart and an
extraordinarily gifted actor,

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:27.866
one of the best, if not the
best, of our generation.

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:29.735
I have so much
admiration for him.

00:04:29.735 --> 00:04:32.171
And, yeah, you know,
there we are as brothers

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:33.672
suddenly just thrown together

00:04:33.672 --> 00:04:35.875
for a brief moment of
trying to create this story.

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:38.043
And these guys,
odd as it seems to us now,

00:04:38.043 --> 00:04:39.512
shared the same bedroom.

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.414
Not the same bed.
We didn't nap in the same bed.

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.849
But, you know,
what is it to sort of lie

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:46.118
beside one another
in those sort of --

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:47.586
everything that
sleep is about,

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:48.721
to be something
that's sort of shared

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:50.323
in such an intimate space.

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:52.158
And, you know,
I've grown up with the guy.

00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:54.060
I've knocked about with him.
We've fought.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.130
Everything that brothers do
over their lifetime.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.031
And I was just meeting this
very cool dude

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:01.567
that I was full of
admiration for, off an airplane.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.404
And, you know, to be thrown
together in such a peculiar way

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:05.838
was actually
really, really helpful.

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:08.641
It just broke that down
and made it something real

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:09.775
and connected.

00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:13.145
And, yeah, all preciousness
just evaporated.

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:14.146
It was great.

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:15.314
-It so funny to me.

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:17.617
And, again,
it speaks to your great range

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.451
as an actor to work on
a project like this

00:05:19.451 --> 00:05:21.320
where it's very much, like,
the authenticity

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.289
of learning another
actor's smell

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:25.258
versus you mentioned
a Marvel movie,

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:26.592
where you show up,
and it's like,

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.293
"Now pretend that over there
is a monster."

00:05:28.293 --> 00:05:29.662
-I know.

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.364
"There's the person you smell.
It's a Ping-Pong ball."

00:05:32.364 --> 00:05:33.733
Yeah, I know.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:36.936
But, you know, I think it still
requires the same kind of

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:39.872
level of interrogation
and authenticity.

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:43.309
You're just using
your imagination a lot more.

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.244
Not least because,
you know, you can't afford

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:49.748
to fix it on a post when you're
doing a film like Jane's.

00:05:49.748 --> 00:05:52.684
And, you know,
the real-world environment

00:05:52.684 --> 00:05:54.921
is a gift in that sense for
"The Power of the Dog."

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:58.991
And, also, the sort of childish
game play and just freedom

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:01.593
of imagination for doing the
sort of Marvel work is --

00:06:01.593 --> 00:06:02.995
It's a different muscle,

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:05.030
but it's equally as interesting
and challenging for me.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:06.732
I love both. I really do.

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:08.934
-You seem like someone that
would be great to

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:10.569
collaborate on a film set,

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:13.339
and, yet, I heard a story
that early in your acting days,

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:15.507
you were in a school play
as a young man.

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:18.778
You were playing Joseph
and you thought the actor

00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:20.780
who was playing Mary was
taking too long

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:22.715
with her line
and you shoved her offstage.

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:23.950
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.518
True story?

00:06:26.518 --> 00:06:27.519
-Yeah.
-Yeah. Okay.

00:06:27.519 --> 00:06:29.755
Yeah, true story.

00:06:29.755 --> 00:06:33.726
And this voice came across an
outdoor swimming pool

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:37.864
in Hampstead, near where I live,
only last year,

00:06:37.864 --> 00:06:39.665
going, "Benedict, Benedict,

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:43.002
I was the Mary you
pushed off the stage

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:44.603
when we were kids."

00:06:44.603 --> 00:06:45.937
You know,
this fully grown adult.

00:06:45.937 --> 00:06:48.507
And I said, "I don't know where
to put myself right now.

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:49.541
I'm so, so sorry."

00:06:49.541 --> 00:06:50.576
And she went, "Oh, no.
It's great.

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.945
I dine out on the story."
So...

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:54.580
-Yeah, I guess that's true.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.282
I mean, if you're not going
to make it as an actor yourself,

00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:57.616
you might as well
get shoved by someone

00:06:57.616 --> 00:06:58.784
who's been
incredibly successful.

00:06:58.784 --> 00:07:01.187
-No, I would not --
Yeah, I wouldn't advocate

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.522
that as a way into a career.

00:07:03.522 --> 00:07:06.492
-So, then you -- Obviously,
you go on to drama school.

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:08.794
What kind of student
were you then?

00:07:08.794 --> 00:07:11.663
-Um...mixed.

00:07:11.663 --> 00:07:13.966
You know, I had great respect
for a lot of the process,

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:15.734
a lot of the classes,
and then, at other times,

00:07:15.734 --> 00:07:18.737
I think I hadn't
got being the sort of

00:07:18.737 --> 00:07:20.139
student who liked to
sort of muck about

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:21.273
and make his friends laugh

00:07:21.273 --> 00:07:22.942
at the back of the class
out of my system.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:24.343
And, you know,
there were moments

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:26.011
of great integrity and stuff
that I learned,

00:07:26.011 --> 00:07:28.647
great pearls of wisdom
and just shards of things

00:07:28.647 --> 00:07:31.016
that just I exorcise
all the time in my work,

00:07:31.016 --> 00:07:33.485
and then other moments
where I was going,

00:07:33.485 --> 00:07:36.254
"This is just -- You know,
I need to sort of do something

00:07:36.254 --> 00:07:37.656
to not get bored and just

00:07:37.656 --> 00:07:39.926
entertain people
at the back of the room."

00:07:39.926 --> 00:07:42.295
So, I would say a mixed bag.

00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:43.429
But I'm very proud of my school.

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:45.331
I'm president of LAMDA now.

00:07:45.331 --> 00:07:48.100
And, you know,
I think there's lots of things

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.334
I'm grateful for

00:07:49.334 --> 00:07:51.404
and the privilege of
my upbringing and my life,

00:07:51.404 --> 00:07:54.073
but one of the things
I notice most is,

00:07:54.073 --> 00:07:55.541
at the beginning of
an actor's career,

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:57.375
every single advantage counts.

00:07:57.375 --> 00:07:59.979
And so we're trying really hard
to make that school

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:03.549
as accessible
and as inclusive as possible.

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:06.085
So that's a thing
I'm really proud of

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:08.687
being part of and being back
in that environment to do.

00:08:08.687 --> 00:08:12.458
But, yeah, at the time, yeah,
I could be a pain in the ass.

00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:14.993
-Very nice, very adult of you to
be able to admit that now.

00:08:14.993 --> 00:08:17.629
You also have a show
coming out on Amazon Prime

00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:19.097
called
"The Electrical Life of --"

00:08:19.097 --> 00:08:20.733
am I saying this right? --
"Louis Wain"?

00:08:20.733 --> 00:08:24.203
-Yes, you are saying it right.
You are saying it right.

00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:25.504
I'm super-proud of this film.

00:08:25.504 --> 00:08:29.108
The director and re-writer,
I should say --

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:30.108
It was Simon Stephenson
originally,

00:08:30.108 --> 00:08:32.144
but it's Will Sharpe.

00:08:32.144 --> 00:08:33.545
He's an extraordinary talent.

00:08:33.545 --> 00:08:35.714
And, rather like Jane,
he just has a master of

00:08:35.714 --> 00:08:38.316
every aspect of filmmaking,
from the ratio of the screen to

00:08:38.316 --> 00:08:41.887
the texture of the clothing to,
I mean, you know,

00:08:41.887 --> 00:08:43.989
even going up to the first
five minutes in the recording

00:08:43.989 --> 00:08:47.325
of his brother's score --
it's a very talented family --

00:08:47.325 --> 00:08:49.227
and saying, "This is what
the character's feeling

00:08:49.227 --> 00:08:52.631
at the moment that
you're playing this theme."

00:08:52.631 --> 00:08:54.800
And it was just --
I fell in love with Louis.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.237
He's a very quiet hero
in a loud Victorian world.

00:08:58.237 --> 00:09:00.472
And he suffered a great deal,

00:09:00.472 --> 00:09:02.107
finding it difficult to be
in this world.

00:09:02.107 --> 00:09:03.942
He was diagnosed
in his life with schizophrenia,

00:09:03.942 --> 00:09:07.914
though he's not here now,
obviously, to ask or observe.

00:09:07.914 --> 00:09:11.150
So, out of respect, we're less
prescriptive in our take on him.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.985
But, you know, he struggled.

00:09:12.985 --> 00:09:15.287
And it's really a film
about love,

00:09:15.287 --> 00:09:16.388
the love he had for his wife,

00:09:16.388 --> 00:09:17.590
beautifully played
by Claire Foy,

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:19.592
sustaining him throughout
his life and his career.

00:09:19.592 --> 00:09:21.427
And -- Oh, gosh.
I don't know.

00:09:21.427 --> 00:09:22.561
I mean, there are just
some characters

00:09:22.561 --> 00:09:23.662
that don't leave you alone.

00:09:23.662 --> 00:09:25.363
I loved Phil Burbank,
perversely,

00:09:25.363 --> 00:09:29.334
'cause he's a troubled dude,
but, Louis Wain --

00:09:29.334 --> 00:09:31.337
There's just -- Yeah, I've got
a real soft spot for him.

00:09:31.337 --> 00:09:32.938
I think he's
a very special human being

00:09:32.938 --> 00:09:34.540
and a story worth telling.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:35.607
-Well, I can't wait to see that.

00:09:35.607 --> 00:09:38.010
And congratulations again
on the film.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:39.478
It really is -- It is a film.

00:09:39.478 --> 00:09:41.981
We were talking briefly
before the show.

00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:43.682
And I know it sounds
a little pretentious,

00:09:43.682 --> 00:09:45.517
but it is really
a piece of cinema

00:09:45.517 --> 00:09:49.888
and it's incredibly engaging and
it's a wonderful story.

00:09:49.888 --> 00:09:51.123
But congratulations.

00:09:51.123 --> 00:09:52.925
